Bounded work: at most 32 capstones are grouped

`detect_grids` finds every capstone in one pass over the image, then
groups them: for each capstone, every pair of plausible neighbours is
tried as a code's other two corners, each on a copy of the whole image
(`find_groupings`). The cost grows with the count squared times the
image's size, and the count is the image's to choose. A 640x480 frame
tiled with 7-pixel finder patterns, 1600 of them, took 25 s on a desktop
CPU; 3500 did not finish in two minutes. A phone scans such a frame from
the camera, whatever it is pointed at.

A frame with more than MAX_CAPSTONES (32, quirc's own QUIRC_MAX_CAPSTONES)
now gives no grids, and the capstone pass itself, a few milliseconds for
those same frames, is all it costs. A camera frame with one or two codes
in it has three capstones each and seldom a false one; a frame over the
limit is skipped, and the next frame is another try.
